<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mastanabal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">2nd century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mastanabal was a Numidian prince, one of the three legitimate sons of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masinissa">Masinissa</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-aemilianus">Scipio Aemilianus</a> divided the royal functions after Masinissa's death in 148 BCE, Mastanabal received responsibility for justice. He was the father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jugurtha">Jugurtha</a>, by a concubine, and of Gauda, both later kings of Numidia, and he died before his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/micipsa">Micipsa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mastanabal.jpg" alt="Mastanabal" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zde · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bronze_head,_north_African,_300_BC,_Cyrene,_BM_Bronze_268,_143107.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mastanabal was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masinissa">Masinissa</a>, king of Numidia, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> kingdom in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>. His name is written MSTNB in Numidian and mstnʿbʾ in Punic; the linguist Salem Chaker has suggested it may be reconstructed as Amastan (a)B(a), perhaps meaning defender or protector of (a)b(a).</p><p>He was well versed in Greek literature and studied law. From his youth he was a keen horseman and athlete, kept a stud of purebred horses and drove in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> races. Around 168 or 164 BCE he won the chariot race at the hippodrome of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panathenaea">Panathenaic Games</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masinissa">Masinissa</a>'s death in 148 BCE the three legitimate sons ruled Numidia together under their father's will, with the help of the Roman general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-aemilianus">Scipio Aemilianus</a>. By Scipio's own account, Mastanabal was then given authority over the administration of Numidia. According to the Greek historian Appian, in his Roman History, Mastanabal shared power with his brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/micipsa">Micipsa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulussa">Gulussa</a> and took charge of judicial affairs. While his elder brother Gulussa led a cavalry force to join the Roman forces fighting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-punic-war">Third Punic War</a>, Mastanabal stayed in Numidia with Micipsa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The Roman historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sallust">Sallust</a>, in his War of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jugurtha">Jugurtha</a>, says that Mastanabal died of an illness. His son Jugurtha was later adopted by his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/micipsa">Micipsa</a> and made co-heir with Micipsa's own sons, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adherbal">Adherbal</a> and Hiempsal. Jugurtha afterwards had both of them put to death in a coup d'état in order to remain sole ruler. Mastanabal's other son, Gauda, born of a second marriage, was named as a subsidiary heir to the kingdom.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mastanabal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On his death in 148 BCE Scipio Aemilianus, acting for Rome, divided royal functions among his three legitimate sons: Micipsa held Cirta and the government, Gulussa the army and <b>Mastanabal</b> justice.</blockquote><a href="/empire/numidia">Numidia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirta">Cirta</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.37,6.61&amp;year=-135" aria-label="Cirta on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Mastanabal"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5454 4443h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5454 4443h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.37,6.61&amp;year=-135">What was here in 135 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
